<Base title="About & copyright" description="How the Classical Urdu Library chooses public-domain books, where the scans come from, and how the translations are made.">
  <h1>About &amp; copyright</h1>
  <p>The Classical Urdu Library publishes classical Urdu books with the original text and a faithful English translation side by side, plus free PDF and EPUB downloads.</p>
  <h2>Which books are included</h2>
  <p>Only works by authors who died in 1961 or earlier are included. Their works are in the public domain in Pakistan (author's life plus 50 years) and India (author's life plus 60 years). Scans come only from freely downloadable sources such as the Internet Archive. Each book's page names the exact scan, edition and year it was made from.</p>
  <p>Modern editions sometimes add introductions, notes or editorial changes that are still under copyright. That material is left out. Only the author's own text is reproduced.</p>
  <h2>United States</h2>
  <p>In the United States, a work first published in 1931 or later can still be under copyright even if its author died long ago. Such books are marked for checking before they are published here.</p>
  <h2>How the translations are made</h2>
  <p>The Urdu text is transcribed page by page from the scan and checked against it. The English stays as close to the original as clear modern English allows. Nothing is summarised or skipped. Poetry is translated couplet by couplet, two lines for two lines, and accuracy comes before rhyme. Key Urdu and Persian terms are kept in italics with a short gloss, and each book has a glossary. Translator's notes are marked <em>[TN: …]</em>, and unreadable passages are marked <em>[illegible]</em>.</p>
  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>If you find a mistake in a transcription or translation, please open an issue on the project's GitHub repository.</p>
</Base>
